---
/**
 * The claims test, where the home page used to compare frameworks. The
 * comparison answers a buyer's question (what do these standards add to the
 * GDPR, the AI Act, NIST, ISO, and the OECD principles?) and stays on /about
 * and /standards. This answers the question the casebook raises: when an
 * institution describes its system, what would show the description is true?
 *
 * Each claim is set as a contract clause with its exhibit: the record an
 * institution would have to produce. The clauses read the way the standards
 * read, which is the point — the form previews where the requirements live.
 * The rows come from content/institutional-claims, which the About page's
 * table shares, so the two cannot drift.
 */
import { institutionalClaims } from "../../content/institutional-claims";
import SectionBlock from "../SectionBlock.astro";

const exhibitFor = (index: number) => String.fromCharCode(65 + index);
---

<SectionBlock
  id="claims"
  eyebrow="The test"
  title="A description of a system is a claim. Each claim has a record that would back it."
  description="The standards do not say who should hold power. They hold an institution to what it says about itself. Each claim below is set as a clause, with the exhibit an institution would have to produce."
  variant="default"
>
  <ol class="home-clauses">
    {
      institutionalClaims.map((claim, index) => (
        <li class="home-clauses__clause">
          <p class="home-clauses__head">
            <span class="home-clauses__num">
              §{index + 1}
            </span>
            <span class="home-clauses__claim">
              If the system is called {claim.claim}
            </span>
          </p>
          <p class="home-clauses__exhibit">
            <span class="home-clauses__exhibit-label">
              Exhibit {exhibitFor(index)}
            </span>
            — {claim.show}
          </p>
          <p class="home-clauses__authority small muted">
            Asked for by{" "}
            {claim.basis.map((item, basisIndex) => (
              <>
                <a href={item.href}>{item.label}</a>
                {basisIndex < claim.basis.length - 1 && " · "}
              </>
            ))}
          </p>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ol>
  <p class="muted small">
    An election can authorize a program. It does not give the person the program
    decides about a way to contest that decision; standing does. For how these
    standards relate to the GDPR, the EU AI Act, the NIST AI RMF, ISO/IEC 42001,
    and the OECD AI Principles, see the
    <a href="/standards#comparison">standards comparison</a>.
  </p>
</SectionBlock>
